  "slug": "untitled-topic-29c0",
  "summary": "Recent preprints test reproducibility, noise handling and cross-modality validation in omics-based drug-response and diagnostic models.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is reproducibility and noise-aware validation in quantitative omics and drug-response modelling. A cross-resource comparison of drug-perturbation transcriptomes finds high agreement for treated expression but limited concordance of drug-induced responses across sources. A glioblastoma study shows that generative augmentation of a 38-patient circulating miRNA cohort fails to generalize across plasma and tissue cohorts, indicating that same-modality validation overstates performance. Other preprints address method-level noise: Elflow automates quality control and self-supervised representation learning for flow and mass cytometry, DeepCINET models viability assay noise in cancer cell line drug-response ranking, and BI-SCUDO Regression reports outperforming LASSO, Random Forest and XGBoost on noisy TCGA omics for survival prediction. PTM-BDL extends drug-response prediction to site-resolved phosphorylation features. The shared novelty lies in explicit noise and cross-dataset testing rather than in single-cohort accuracy.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
